## Coding principles

Apply when writing or changing code, configs, migrations, and tests.

### Simplicity

- Write the smallest correct solution. No abstraction until a second real caller exists.
- Keep related logic together; do not spread one behavior across modules.

### Locality

- Keep helpers, types, constants, and components in the file that uses them until a second file needs them.
- Do not create a new file, module, or folder for single-use code.

### Non-negotiable safety

- Validate and type every input crossing a trust boundary: HTTP body, query params, env, webhooks, file uploads, third-party responses.
- Never hardcode secrets, tokens, or credentials. Read them from env or the secret store.
- Never build SQL, shell commands, or HTML by concatenating untrusted input — use parameters, safe APIs, or escaping.
- Never log secrets, tokens, passwords, or full personal data.
- Enforce authorization on the server for every protected action. A hidden UI control is not access control.

### Errors and failure

- Handle the error or let it propagate with context. Never catch and continue silently.
- Every external call — network, database, filesystem — needs a timeout and a defined behavior on failure.
- Log at the point of failure with enough context to debug it without a reproduction.
- Error messages name what failed and the identifying value; keep internals out of user-facing text.

### Order of attention within one change

Make it correct, then make it safe and observable, then simplify. This orders attention inside one change — not permission to ship the first step alone.

### Hygiene

- Add or update tests for behavior you changed; at minimum, cover the failure path you introduced.
- Leave touched code cleaner than you found it, within the scope you were given.
- One canonical place per fact, constant, or type — no drifting copies.
- Delete commented-out code and dead branches; git remembers.

### Do not

- Refactor code outside the requested change.
- Add a dependency without saying why in your summary.
- Suppress type errors, lint rules, or failing tests to make a check pass.
- Leave TODOs, stubs, or placeholder returns in code you report as done.
